      "text": "Let $x$ be the scalling factor and let $a$, $b$, and $c$ represent the lengths of the sides of the similar triangle. We want the longest side of the similar triangle.\nBy similarity of triangles, we have $$\\frac{9}{x}=\\frac{7}{x-2} = \\frac{9}{x+2}$$\nThe ratio $\\frac{9}{9+x}$ must be equal to the ratio of the corresponding sides of similar triangles. Equating the corresponding side lengths we have $\\frac{9}{9+x}=\\frac{7}{7+x}$. This equation can be simplified to $9(7+x)=7(9+x)$. This, in turn, simplifies to $63+9x=63+7x$ which is equal to $2x =0$, therefore $x=0$. $x$ cannot equal zero. Then, we have $\\frac{9}{9+x}=\\frac{7}{7+x}$ which is true for any $x$. Recall that $x$ represents a scaling factor. This means that $7$ must be the longest side in the first triangle, since it represents the side with the largest ratio.\nSince $7$ is the longest side of the first triangle, then it must be the longest side of the similar triangle.  \nThus, the length of the longest side is $7$ cm. We have that it must be $\\boxed{7}$ centimeters.The answer is: 7",
